changelog: |
  0.2.0 — the shipper starts again under Filebeat 9.

  filebeat.fleet.yml used the `container` input, a preset of the `log` input
  that Filebeat 9 no longer starts. Under the pinned 9.5.3 every labelled
  container failed the autodiscover config check ("Container input is
  deprecated. Use Filestream input with its container parser instead") and no
  container log reached the plane: 259 such errors on the hub from 2026-10-17
  to 2026-11-09, and no `logs-fleet.container-*` data stream.

  * The input is now `filestream` with a per-container id, the `container`
    parser (Docker's json-file wrapper) and then `ndjson` with the same three
    options the old `json.*` keys carried (keys at the root, an error key on a
    line that is not JSON, `msg` as the message key).
  * `ignore_decoding_error: true`: a plain-text line still ships with its raw
    `message` and the error key; Filebeat no longer also writes one error to
    its own log for each.
  * Checked against elastic/filebeat:9.5.3 over a sample json-file log: the old
    payload exits at start, the new one ships every line.
  * 0.1.0 is in archive/, so `--upgrade` still recognises a deployed copy.

  0.1.0 — the log plane reaches the fleet.

  The hub grew a real log plane (Elasticsearch/Logstash/Kibana + Grafana behind
  `docker compose --profile elk`, docs/OBSERVABILITY.md). This kit is the other
  half: what a repo needs to PRODUCE logs that plane can read, and to stand up
  its own single-node copy when it is cloned outside the hub.

  * filebeat.fleet.yml — THE VENDORED PAYLOAD. One shipper config fleet-wide,
    byte-identical to the hub's tools/observability/filebeat/filebeat.yml and
    held there by drift check (i). A drifted copy is worse than a missing one:
    the repo still ships, just in a different shape, into a shared index.
  * compose.labels.yml — the com.bamr87.fleet.* labels and json-file rotation
    (UPS-OPS-17). The labels ARE the opt-in: Filebeat autodiscovers on them and
    ignores everything else, so a service joins the log plane by labelling
    itself rather than by an edit anywhere central.
  * compose.elk.yml — a standalone single-node Elasticsearch + Kibana +
    Filebeat overlay, so a clone outside the hub gets a log UI instead of
    nothing. Inside the hub the same labels feed the hub's one stack and this
    overlay stays unused.
  * adapters/ — UPS-OPS-10 JSON emission per stack, each with the UPS-OPS-12
    redaction filter attached. Structured emission is the MUST; this kit only
    makes it convenient.
  * tests/contract.test.mjs — asserts an emitted line carries every field the
    contract names and that the redaction actually redacts.
notes: |
  - Seeding is ADDITIVE. The kit never edits a hand-written docker-compose.yml;
    the seeder prints the one `include:` (or per-service `labels:`) line a human
    adds, exactly as the feedback kit prints its mount line.
  - Inside the hub, do NOT run compose.elk.yml — the hub already runs one
    stack and a second Elasticsearch will fight it for port 9200 and for RAM.
  - EDIT THE HUB COPY FIRST (tools/observability/filebeat/filebeat.yml), then
    re-vendor. Drift check (i) compares the two.
  - Snapshot the outgoing filebeat.fleet.yml into archive/ BEFORE editing it,
    or every deployed copy reads as hand-modified and --upgrade stops reaching
    the fleet (the trap templates/agent-context/VERSION documents).
